{"reference":"Malachi 1","book":"Malachi","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions and Replies","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Much of this chapter moves through a disputation pattern: a divine assertion is met by an audience question, followed by an extended answer. The opening claim of love is challenged in verses 2–5, and the charge that priests despise God’s name is challenged in verses 6–8. This form makes the chapter’s accusations specific rather than abstract: the response about Jacob and Esau addresses the first question, while the examples of blemished animals—including the comparison with a governor—address the second. The chapter then contrasts such defective worship with the declaration that God’s name will be great among the nations. The heading says the word comes through “Malachi,” but the book itself gives no biography and does not settle whether this is a personal name, a shortened name, or a title meaning “my messenger.”","verse_refs":["Malachi 1:1","Malachi 1:10-14","Malachi 1:2-14","Malachi 1:2-5","Malachi 1:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_001_642654996c9587b2111c3f2d"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Setting of the Worship Critique","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter’s priests, altar offerings, and reference to a governor fit a setting that is commonly considered likely to be Persian-period Yehud. The governor comparison is an administrative analogy: if an official would not accept a blind, lame, or sick gift, the passage presses the question of why such offerings are being brought to God. The criticism focuses on the priests’ treatment of the altar and its food as contemptible, presenting this as dishonor to God’s name.","verse_refs":["Malachi 1:1","Malachi 1:6-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_001_8d5103c150315f23d4654855"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}